title: MeasurementsQuest1
tags:
---
The radio static is louder and clearer here than ever before. You can make up words, yet they sound mixed up.
Random nonsense garbled by inconvenient static buzzes and half-heard syllables.
This spot must be what Dr. Aaldenburg was talking about. One of those measurement spots.
-> Take out the GPS
    You take the GPS out of your pocket, and turn it on. After a few seconds, the screen comes to live.
    Two numbers, with an obscene amount of decimals are shown on it. This must be the coordinates.
    Well they don't call it busy work for nothing.

-> Note down the numbers and put the GPS away.
    One down, two to go. At least now you know better what to look for.

===

title: MeasurementsQuest2
tags:
---
The static reaches a familiar peak here in volume and clarity. Same as the previous time, this is the spot.
-> Take out the GPS
    You take the GPS from your pocket. You left it on this time, though judging by the battery symbol it may have been a mistake.
    The new coordinates are visible on the screen, same tedious amount of decimals to copy.

-> Note down the numbers and put the GPS away. After turning it off this time.
    Just one more measurement to take, and you can go back to Nauka square. No time to dilly-dally.

===

title: MeasurementsQuest3
tags:
---
You recognise another perfect measurement spot right away. Your radio is going crazy. The final one, at last.
-> Take out the GPS
    You take out the GPS from your pocket. It's strange that you may never hold this in your hand again after this.
    What a curious device. You wonder what the world may look like, if such technology could be applied worldwide.
    That makes you think, did Aaldenburg just use this as an excuse to get you to test the GPS? Does it even matter?

-> Note down the final coordinates
    Time to head back to the lab, and hopefully, get some answers.


===